      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I can confirm that the Unity Cloth scaling issue is reproducible again in the current Unity 6-based VRChat client.\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n\n1. Wear an avatar with a Unity Cloth-based skirt.\n\n2. Use VRChat's built-in Avatar Height adjustment to change the avatar's size.\n\n3. The avatar's body scales correctly, but the Cloth mesh does not scale proportionally, causing the clothing to appear incorrectly sized.\n\nThis happens **without switching avatars**.\n\n**Expected behavior:**\n\nThe Cloth mesh should scale correctly with the rest of the avatar.\n\n**Actual behavior:**\n\nThe Cloth mesh does not properly reflect the avatar's new scale, resulting in incorrect clothing proportions.\n\n**Regression history:**\n\n- VRChat 2023.3.2 and 2023.4.2 included fixes for Cloth scaling.\n\n- A related Unity 2022 Open Beta report was marked Complete on April 22, 2024.\n\n- The issue now appears to have returned following the migration to Unity 6.\n\nPreviously completed report:\n\nhttps://feedback.vrchat.com/open-beta/p/cloth-applied-mesh-does-not-scale-when-avatar-scale-is-changed\n\n**Potentially related issue:**\n\nAnother tracked report describes how switching from a small avatar to a normal-height avatar can cause a Cloth-based skirt to become significantly shorter, apparently depending on the previously used avatar's height.\n\nhttps://feedback.vrchat.com/bug-reports/p/1920-cloth-scale-changes-on-last-avatar-height\n\nThese are two distinct reproduction scenarios, but both involve incorrect scaling of Unity Cloth.\n\nCould the development team investigate whether these issues share an underlying cause, and whether the previous Cloth scaling fixes need to be updated for Unity 6?\n\nThank you for looking into this.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
